Here's the xUnit equivalent:

```csharp
// One-time setup/teardown → IClassFixture + a shared fixture class
public class DatabaseFixture : IAsyncLifetime
{
    public Task InitializeAsync()
    {
        Database.Initialize();
        return Task.CompletedTask;
    }

    public Task DisposeAsync()
    {
        Database.Cleanup();
        return Task.CompletedTask;
    }
}

// Per-test setup → constructor; teardown → IDisposable
public class CustomerRepositoryTests : IClassFixture<DatabaseFixture>, IDisposable
{
    private readonly DbContext _context;
    private readonly CustomerRepository _repo;

    public CustomerRepositoryTests(DatabaseFixture fixture)
    {
        // Runs before each test (replaces [SetUp])
        _context = new TestDbContext();
        _repo = new CustomerRepository(_context);
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        // Runs after each test (replaces [TearDown])
        _context.Dispose();
    }

    [Fact]
    public void FindById_ExistingCustomer_ReturnsCustomer()
    {
        var customer = _repo.FindById(1);
        Assert.NotNull(customer);
        Assert.Equal("Alice", customer.Name);
    }
}
```

**Key mappings:**

| NUnit | xUnit |
|-------|-------|
| `[OneTimeSetUp/TearDown]` | `IClassFixture<T>` with fixture class implementing `IAsyncLifetime` or `IDisposable` |
| `[SetUp]` | Constructor |
| `[TearDown]` | `IDisposable.Dispose()` |
| `[TestFixture]` | (removed — not needed) |
| `[Test]` | `[Fact]` |

The fixture instance is shared across all tests in the class and injected via the constructor. xUnit creates a **new class instance per test**, so the constructor/Dispose naturally handle per-test lifecycle.
